## 1. Overview: The Three Tools and Their Unity {#1-overview}

Exception Theory provides three operational tools that together form the complete methodology for understanding any phenomenon in existence. These are not heuristics, metaphors, or guidelines — they are formal consequences of the PDT axiom system, derived forward from the three irreducible primitives.

The three tools are:

**The Identification Principle** — the universal diagnostic for understanding. It tells you *what* must be found: the complete PDT decomposition of the phenomenon under investigation. It answers the question: "Is my understanding complete, and if not, what is missing?"

**The Descriptor Gap Principle** — the universal search procedure. It tells you *how* to find what is missing: any gap in a description is itself a Descriptor waiting to be identified. It answers the question: "Where do I look next?"

**The Subsumption Law** — the universal completeness criterion. It tells you *when* you are finished: a primitive, a category, or a description is complete if and only if it subsumes everything within its domain without remainder. It answers the question: "Am I done?"

Together they form a closed operational loop:

$$\text{Identify} \xrightarrow{\text{gap found}} \text{Search} \xrightarrow{\text{gap closed}} \text{Verify completeness} \xrightarrow{\text{incomplete}} \text{Identify again}$$

This loop terminates when subsumption is achieved — when every feature of the phenomenon is captured by the PDT decomposition with no remainder. The Verification Principle (mathematical consistency indicates sufficient Descriptors) provides the empirical test for termination.

