## 7. Worked Examples Across Domains {#7-worked-examples}

### 7.1 Physics: Gravity

**Step 1 — Identification Principle:**

| Primitive | Identification |
|---|---|
| P_gravity | Spacetime — the substrate manifold where gravitational effects occur |
| D_gravity | Mass-energy distribution, curvature tensor, gravitational coupling constant (G), field equations |
| T_gravity | Gravitational agency — the T-type binding that navigates mass-energy Descriptors, producing the attraction we observe |

**Step 2 — Descriptor Gap Principle:** The Standard Model treats gravity as a force but cannot quantize it. The gap: a missing Descriptor at the interface between quantum D-structure and gravitational D-structure. In ET terms, the gap is the missing Descriptor that bridges the d=1 (octave, gravitational) sublattice and the quantum sublattices (d=2, d=3, d=4). This gap — quantum gravity — is itself a Descriptor waiting to be identified.

**Step 3 — Subsumption:** Every instance of gravitational agency — orbital motion, free fall, tidal forces, gravitational lensing, gravitational waves, galaxy rotation — is T_gravity. No gravitational phenomenon has been found that requires a separate agency. Subsumption holds.

### 7.2 Consciousness

**Step 1 — Identification Principle:**

| Primitive | Identification |
|---|---|
| P_consciousness | The neural substrate — the brain, the body, the physical configuration that supports conscious experience |
| D_consciousness | Thoughts, emotions, memories, beliefs, sensory qualia, neural activation patterns — the Descriptors of conscious content |
| T_consciousness | T itself — the Traverser in the act of self-observation. Attention, will, choice, awareness. The agency that navigates the D-space of conscious content |

**Step 2 — Descriptor Gap Principle:** The "hard problem of consciousness" is, in ET terms, the failure to identify T_consciousness as categorically irreducible to D_consciousness. The gap: prior frameworks treat consciousness as emergent from physical Descriptors (neural correlates), which is an attempt to reduce T to D. The Subsumption Law proves this cannot work — T cannot be subsumed by D. The gap closes when T is recognized as a primitive, not a product.

**Step 3 — Subsumption:** Every aspect of conscious experience — perception, volition, self-reflection, dreaming, altered states — maps to one of P, D, or T. No aspect of consciousness has been found that requires a fourth category. Subsumption holds.

### 7.3 The Digital-Computational Domain

**Step 1 — Identification Principle:**

| Primitive | Identification |
|---|---|
| P_digital | The space of all possible binary configurations: {0,1}* — the Cantor space. The address space, not the silicon. |
| D_digital | Instruction sets (ISA), data types, protocols, formats, OS primitives, programming language grammars, compilers, network standards |
| T_digital | The CPU clock cycle — the agency that reads instruction (D), modifies memory state (P∘D), and advances. Also: the programmer, the user, the network packet — every agent navigating D-structured binary P-space |

**Step 2 — Descriptor Gap Principle:** Security vulnerabilities are Descriptor Gaps — configurations where the D-set (permissions, validation rules, access controls) is incomplete, allowing T (an attacker) to navigate to configurations the designer did not intend. Every vulnerability is a missing Descriptor; every patch is a Descriptor added.

**Step 3 — Subsumption:** Every computational phenomenon — algorithms, data structures, operating systems, networks, databases, AI models, user interfaces — maps to P∘D∘T configurations. No computational phenomenon requires a fourth primitive. Subsumption holds.

### 7.4 Temperature (Real-Feel)

**Step 1 — Identification Principle:**

| Primitive | Identification |
|---|---|
| P_temperature | The undifferentiated thermal substrate — the bare capacity to hold thermal potential |
| D_temperature | Temperature, humidity, wind speed, dewpoint, solar radiation — the Descriptors of the thermal environment |
| T_temperature | The human body's thermoregulatory agency — the physiological traverser navigating the thermal D-space and producing the subjective "feels like" experience |

**Step 2 — Descriptor Gap Principle:** Initial model with only three Descriptors (temperature, humidity, wind speed) had gaps. Error patterns revealed two missing Descriptors (dewpoint, solar radiation). Adding them closed the gap. Model error → 0.

**Step 3 — Subsumption:** All aspects of thermal experience — sweating, shivering, thermal comfort, sunburn risk, frostbite — are captured by the complete D-set bound to the thermal P-substrate, navigated by T. No remainder.

### 7.5 A Chemical Reaction

**Step 1 — Identification Principle:**

| Primitive | Identification |
|---|---|
| P_reaction | The configuration space of all possible molecular arrangements of the reactant atoms |
| D_reaction | Bond energies, activation energy, temperature, pressure, catalyst properties, molecular geometries, electron configurations |
| T_reaction | The transition-state agency — the process that navigates from the reactant Descriptor set to the product Descriptor set through the activation energy barrier |

**Step 2 — Descriptor Gap Principle:** If the predicted reaction rate does not match the observed rate, a Descriptor is missing. Historically, this gap led to the discovery of catalysis (a missing D: the catalyst's surface geometry and electronic structure), enzyme specificity (missing D: the active site's shape), and quantum tunneling effects in reactions (missing D: the tunneling probability).

**Step 3 — Subsumption:** Every chemical phenomenon — bonding, reaction kinetics, equilibria, catalysis, spectroscopy — maps to P∘D∘T. No chemical phenomenon requires a fourth primitive.

